    "taxonId": 69936,
    "summary": "A deciduous tree whose precise origin is disputed, with proposals spanning Central and West Asia; recent sources particularly identify Iran and Anatolia. Its fragrant white or pale-pink flowers appear before the leaves, and its fruit has a leathery hull and hard shell surrounding the edible seed.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The seed is eaten raw or toasted and used in dishes; bitter almonds contain dangerous cyanide, which must be removed before consumption.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p21", "s1:p8", "s1:p10"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p73", "s1:p55"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 69974,
    "summary": "A genus of flowering trees and shrubs, mostly from Neotropical and Afrotropical regions. Each fleshy fruit combines many smaller fruits, while the seed kernels are toxic. Seven named species and the atemoya hybrid are cultivated mostly for edible fruits, and several other species also bear edible fruit.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Seven named species and atemoya are cultivated mostly for edible fruits; A. crassiflora and A. salzmannii also produce edible fruit.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p8"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p4"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 70025,
    "summary": "A genus of palm-like trees and shrubs native to the Old World tropics and subtropics. Many have crowns of strap-shaped leaves and thick stilt roots supporting their top-heavy growth; their fruits comprise pineapple-like sections that typically turn orange or red when ripe.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Some species provide food; ripe large- and medium-fruited forms are eaten raw or cooked, while partly ripe fruit should be cooked.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p6"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p20", "s1:p29"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 70027,
    "summary": "A tropical flowering tree from a Pacific source, believed to have been selectively bred in Polynesia from breadnut. It has large, deeply cut leaves, and every part yields latex. Its fruit is a staple in many tropical regions; ripe and unripe fruit are used, but unripe fruit is cooked before eating.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Ripe fruit may be eaten raw or cooked; unripe fruit is cooked. Fruit is also dried into flour, and seeds are cooked.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p3", "s1:p7", "s1:p19", "s1:p24"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p24", "s1:p26"]
    }
  },
